!! |> Section

!!     *Function operations and signatures.*
!!     When we take the sum...

!!     $$
!!     \Large f + g
!!     $$

!!     ...of two functions, the same input, when evaluating the
!!     sum, is fed to both functions:

!!     $$
!!     \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x).
!!     $$

!!     This means that in order for the sum to make sense 
!!     !! at all, 
!!     the functions must take inputs with the same
!!     units; in addition, by the old adage apples should not be added
!!     to oranges, the two functions must output values with the
!!     same units as well; in other words, if $f$ and $g$ have
!!     signatures...

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{gathered}
!!     f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}] \\
!!     g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
!!     \end{gathered}
!!     $$

!!     ...then taking the sum...

!!     $$
!!     \Large f + g
!!     $$

!!     ...implies the constraints...

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{aligned}
!!     \te{A} &= \te{C} \\
!!     \te{B} &= \te{D} \up{1.35}
!!     \end{aligned}
!!     $$

!!     ...on the units in order for the sum to be “well-formed”, not
!!     nonsensical!; i.e., the most general signature under which
!!     a sum of $f$ and $g$ makes sense is...

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{aligned}
!!     f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]& \\
!!     g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]& \up{1.35}
!!     \end{aligned}
!!     $$

!!     ...with the _same_ input units, and the _same_ output
!!     units!

!! |> Section
!!     *Notation.*
!!     We write

!!     $$
!!     \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
!!     $$

!!     to indicate that $f$ is a function $f : \rr \ra \rr$
!!     that takes inputs with units A
!!     and produces outputs with units B. 
!!     For example,

!!     $$
!!     \Large h : [\te{kg}] \ra [^\circ\te{F}]
!!     $$

!!     indicates that $h$ 
!!     is a function whose inputs denote kilograms and whose
!!     outputs denote degrees fahrenheit, etc.
!!     (These are purely “physical” concepts, not
!!     mathematical, but we're cool with that.)

!!     |> Pause

!!     *Vocabulary.* The input and output units of a function,
!!     when present as above, are referred to as the

!!     __signature__

!!     of the function.

!! |> Section
!!     *Compatible signatures for function operations.* 
!!     Taking the sum or product of functions etc
!!     presupposes some “compatibility” between their
!!     signatures—for 

!!     !! |> Pause
!!     !! *Constraints on function signatures imposed by
!!     !! function operations.* Taking the sum, product,
!!     !! quotient, etc, of two functions imposes certain
!!     !! constraints on their signatures, in order for the
!!     !! operation to not be physically meaningless.
!!     !! The following table shows the
!!     !! most general patterns that are possible:
!!     !! of signature possible when taking
!!     !! the sum, product, quotient, or composition of two functions:


!! |> Section
!!     *Function operation and units.* Taking the sum or
!!     product, etc, of two functions imposes certain
!!     constraints on their signatures in order for the operation
!!     to not be physically meaningless, in ways that are
!!     worth being aware of. The following table shows the
!!     most general pattern of signature possible when taking
!!     the sum, product, quotient, or composition of two functions:

    

!!     !! of two functions

!!     !! $$
!!     !! \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)
!!     !! $$
!!     $$
!!     \Large f + g
!!     $$

!!     !! presupposes that the inputs and outputs of
!!     !! $f$ and $g$ meet certain requirements; e.g.,

!!     !! $$
!!     !! \Large 
!!     !! $$

!! |> Section
!!     !! *Function operations.*
!!     *The constraints imposed by function operations
!!     on units.*
!!     When we add two functions the
!!     same input shows up as an input to both functions:

!!     $$
!!     \Large (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x)
!!     $$

!!     What this means is that if units are present 
!!     $f$ and $g$ must both take the _same kind_ of input, units-wise,
!!     in order for the function addition to
!!     make any sense; if we write

!!     $$
!!     \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
!!     $$

!!     !! to indicate that the inputs to $f$ have
!!     !! unit A and the output of $f$ have unit B, then
!!     to indicate that $f$ takes inputs with units A
!!     and produces outputs with units B, and

!!     $$
!!     \Large g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}]
!!     $$

!!     likewise for $g$ for some units C and D, then the sum

!!     $$
!!     \Large f + g
!!     $$

!!     cannot/must not/should not take place unless

!!     $$
!!     \Large \te{A} = \te{C}
!!     $$

!!     because the same input $x$ must have units A
!!     when it is fed to $f$, and units C when it is fed
!!     to $g$! In addition, we must have
!!     !! the constraint
!!     !! to $g$!!!!!!!!!! Though in this case, the constraint

!!     $$
!!     \Large \te{B} = \te{D}
!!     $$

!!     also in this case, by the old addage that apples should
!!     not be added to oranges. (Specifically, note 
!!     that $f(x)$ has units B, that
!!     $g(x)$ has units D, and that $f(x)$ is added to $g(x)$.)
    
!!     By contrast,

!!     __apples can be multiplied by oranges__

!!     as testified by units such as __meters squared__ or __kilowatt-hours__
!!     or __worker years__ or even __meters per second__
!!     that is “meters times per seconds” (or “meters times seconds inverse ($\te{s}^{-1}$)”),
!!     !! (take _meters squared_ or _kilowatt-hours_,
!!     !! or even _meters per second_, where in the last
!!     !! case we multiply _meters_ by 
!!     !! _per seconds_, $\te{s}^{-1}$)
!!     !! and so 
!!     so to form the product of two functions

!!     $$
!!     \Large fg
!!     $$

!!     with signatures 

!!     $$
!!     \begin{gathered}
!!     \,\Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}], \\
!!     \Large g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
!!     \end{gathered}
!!     $$

!!     the constraint 

!!     $$
!!     \Large \te{A} = \te{C}
!!     $$

!!     must still be obeyed (because

!!     $$
!!     \Large (fg)(x) = f(x)g(x)
!!     $$

!!     and ‘$x$’ again appears as an input to both $f$ and
!!     $g$), but, by  the constraint

!!     $$
!!     \Large \te{B} = \te{D}
!!     $$

!!     can be dropped, because any kind of unit can be
!!     multiplied by any other kind of unit! So in this case
!!     the “general form” of $f$ and $g$ would be

!!     $$
!!     \begin{gathered}
!!     \,\Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}], \\
!!     \,\Large g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{C}]\, \up{1.35}
!!     \end{gathered}
!!     $$

!!     for some units A, B, C, while in the case of function
!!     addition the “general form” would be

!!     $$
!!     \begin{gathered}
!!     \,\Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}], \\
!!     \,\Large g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]\, \up{1.35}
!!     \end{gathered}
!!     $$

!!     with only two degrees of freedom in the units, A and B.

!!     !! $$
!!     !! \Large \te{A} = \te{C}
!!     !! $$

!!     !! remains, while the constraint

!!     !! $$
!!     !! \Large \te{B} = \te{D}
!!     !! $$
    
!!     !! is dropped,
!!     !! by contrast,
!!     !! and the output units B and D can be 
!!     !! completely arbitrary!

!!     !! is lost, for $f : [A] \ra [B]$, $g : [C] \ra [D]$.
!!     !! —because the same input $x$ is used as
!!     !! an input to both $f$ and $g$—but



!!     !! “seconds inverse”,
!!     !! or “one over seconds”, $\te{s}^{-1}$)
!!     !! must also be satisfied in order for the function
!!     !! addition to make any sense, because the outputs
!!     !! $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are added together, and by the
!!     !! old addage about adding apples to oranges.



!!     !! $$
!!     !! \begin{gathered}
!!     !! \Large f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}] \\
!!     !! \Large g : [\te{C}] \ra [\te{D}] \up{1.2}
!!     !! \end{gathered}
!!     !! $$

!! |> Section
!!     *The chain rule.* 
!!     Starting slow, we invite you to verify the following
!!     multiplication:

!!     $$
!!     2.22 \times 10.1 = 22.422.
!!     $$

!!     Here is a multiplication only
!!     of units:

!!     $$
!!     \,\left[{\te{m}\over \te{s}}\right]\times\left[{\te{\$}\over \te{m}}\right] = \left[{\te{\$}\over \te{m}}\right].
!!     $$

!!     We can combine the two:

!!     |> Boxed
!!         $$
!!         2.22[\te{m}/\te{s}] \times 10.1[\te{\$}/\te{m}] = 22.422[\te{\$}/\te{s}]
!!         $$

!!     The multiplication of

!!     __meters per second__

!!     (“[m$/$s]”) by

!!     __dollars per meter__

!!     (“[\$$/$m]”) gives

!!     __dollars per second__

!!     (“[\$$/$s]”) yay!


