    !! “initial constraint” on the
    !! signatures as we do on the left-hand side of the equation!

    !! Firstly, taking the sum

    !! $$
    !! \Large f + g
    !! $$

    !! implies that $f$ and $g$ have signatures
    !! of the form

    !! $$
    !! \Large
    !! \begin{aligned}
    !! f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    !! g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \up{1.35}
    !! \end{aligned}
    !! $$

    !! for some units A, B, as per the rules of Table 1.1; 
    !! then one finds, one by one, that:

    !! $$
    !! \Large
    !! \begin{gathered}
    !! f + g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}] \\
    !! (f + g)' : [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    !! f' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    !! g' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    !! f' + g' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35}
    !! \end{gathered}
    !! $$

    !! ...by the rules according to which function
    !! signatures change or don't change under summation
    !! and differentation; 
    !! in particular, 

    !! $$
    !! \Large (f + g)'
    !! $$

    !! is found to have the same signature as

    !! $$
    !! \Large f' + g'
    !! $$

    !! which is concordant with the sum rule—_we have
    !! at least not debunked the sum rule_ by our
    !! cursory glance at the units!

    !! Though maybe we have another chance at debunking
    !! the rule, as the “initial constraints” 

    !! $$
    !! \Large
    !! \begin{aligned}
    !! f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    !! g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \up{1.35}
    !! \end{aligned}
    !! $$

    !! on the signatures
    !! were deduced by looking at the presence of “$f + g$”
    !! in the sum rule, but which appears in the left-hand
    !! side of the equation only; maybe there is a chance that
    !! the right-hand side of the equation accepts a greater
    !! variety of signatures than the left-hand side,
    !! leading to breakage of the sum rule after all?
    
    !! To double-check against the latter possibility,
    !! i.e., 
    !! to check if the right-hand side doesn't perchance
    !! accept a greater variety of $f$ and $g$ signatures than the
    !! left-hand side,
    !! start by taking
    !! an “agnostic” view on the signatures of $f$ and $g$ on the
    !! right-hand side,
    !! writing

    !! $$
    !! \Large
    !! \begin{aligned}
    !! f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    !! g : [\te{C}] &\ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
    !! \end{aligned}
    !! $$

    !! for some arbitrary units A, B, C, D. Then

    !! $$
    !! \Large
    !! \begin{aligned}
    !! f' : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}/\tA] \\
    !! g' : [\te{C}] &\ra [\te{D}/\tC] \up{1.35}
    !! \end{aligned}
    !! $$

    !! !! but 
    !! and the fact that we are taking the sum

    !! $$
    !! \Large f' + g'
    !! $$

    !! implies 

    !! $$
    !! \Large
    !! \begin{aligned}
    !! \tA &= \tC \\
    !! \tB/\tA &= \tD/\tC \up{1.35}
    !! \end{aligned}
    !! $$

    !! [a.k.a., “the input units of $f'$ equal the input units
    !! of $g'$ and the output units of $f'$ equal the output
    !! units of $g'$”]
    !! which, together, imply 

    !! $$
    !! \Large
    !! \begin{aligned}
    !! \tA &= \tC \\
    !! \tB &= \tD \up{1.35}
    !! \end{aligned}
    !! $$

    !! ...and we recover the same “initial constraint” on the
    !! signatures as we do on the left-hand side of the equation!

    !! (So, the sum rule survives all of our scrutiny.)






